@database noC-WHO Documentation
******************************************************************************
@NODE MAIN "noC-WHO Version 1.60 Complete Guide - by nocTurne deSign/OGN in '94"

            noC-WHO Version 1.6 - (C)1994 nocTurne deSign/Origin
            ----------------------------------------------------
            @{" Overview       " LINK OVER   }....General Description of Features
            @{" Installation   " LINK INSTALL}.....Installing/Configuring noC-WHO
            @{" Usage          " LINK USAGE  }..................Door Usage/Theory
            @{" Style Creation " LINK STYLE  }.....How to create your own designs
            @{" Changes        " LINK CHANGES}.................Whats new for v1.6
            @{" Credits/Greets " LINK CREDS  }..........Where to reach the author
            ----------------------------------------------------

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE OVER "noC-WHO Overview..."

 noC-WHO  is  the  one  of  most  full-featured  and  flexible  WHO  doors
 available, from both the standpoint of the SYSOP and the USER.  Following
 is a list of some of the available features of this release:
 
   · SIMPLE TO INSTALL: Just double click the INSTALL icon.
   · Compatible with Ami-Express V3.3X+ (32 node version),
     3.2X- (10 node version), and Sigma Express.
   · noC-WHO now locates itself into ram, and uses several buffering 
     techniques for swiftest performance.
   · SEVERAL DESIGN STYLES are included for the user to choose from.
   · All styles are fully EDITABLE/CREATABLE.
   · Sysops may appoint PRIVILEGED users who can:
        · Set their name, location, and action strings.
        · See all QUIET users and their actions.
        · Constantly monitor nodes using the update option.
   · noC-WHO may serve MULTIPLE purposes, (frontdoor, etc...)
   · No more 'IN A DOOR' texts.  All doors may be recognized/named.
   · A miscellaneous field may be configured by the sysop.
   · All action texts are configurable.
   · Calls per day are known WITHOUT any additional doors, and are
     reset at midnight automatically.

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE INSTALL "noC-WHO Installation..."

 noC-WHO  is compatible with Ami-Express versions 2.xx thru 3.xx and Sigma
 Express.  To install, simply double-click or execute the included INSTALL
 script.  This will install all files appropriately.

 To   personalize   noC-WHO   to   your   taste,   edit   the    text file
 "DOORS:noC-WHO/who.config".    All  of  the  OPTIONS and DESIGN-PATHS are
 located  in  this  file.   The following parameters may be set within the
 configuration file:
         -----------------------------------------------------------
         @{" FILENAMES.ACS     " link opt1} @{" COOLUSER.[NUM]   " link opt7 } @{" [DOOR].NAME      " link opt13}
         @{" DOORNAMES.ACS     " link opt2} @{" MISC.[NUM]       " link opt8 } @{" [CALL].STYLE     " link opt14}
         @{" PRIV.ACS          " link opt3} @{" SYSOP_ACTION     " link opt9 } @{" TITLE.[NUM]      " link opt15}
         @{" DIVIDERS_DISABLED " link opt4} @{" YOURNODE.TEXT    " link opt10} @{" STYLE_FILE.[NUM] " link opt16}
         @{" CLEAR_SCREEN      " link opt5} @{" SOMEONE.TEXT     " link opt11} @{" @BEGIN_ACTIONS   " link opt17}
         @{" PUBLIC_UPDATE     " link opt6} @{" SOMEWHERE.TEXT   " link opt12} @{" @END_ACTIONS     " link opt18}
         @{" SHOW_QUIET        " link optA} @{" QUIETACTION.TEXT " link optB } @{" SHOW_EMPTY_NODES " link optC }
         -----------------------------------------------------------

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE opt1 "noC-WHO Configuration Option: FILENAMES.ACS"

 @{b}Template:@{ub} FILENAMES.ACS=[NUM]
  @{b}Example:@{ub} FILENAMES.ACS=20

 This is the minimum security level needed to be able to see the names
 of files being transfered.

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE opt2 "noC-WHO Configuration Option: DOORNAMES.ACS"

 @{b}Template:@{ub} DOORNAMES.ACS=[NUM]
  @{b}Example:@{ub} DOORNAMES.ACS=20

 This is the minimum security level needed to be able to see the names
 of doors others are using.

 If you want that alternate be used, (ie "NewFiles V1.5" rather than "N"),
 use the @{"[DOOR].NAME" link opt12} option.

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE opt3 "noC-WHO Configuration Option: PRIV.ACS"

 @{b}Template:@{ub} PRIV.ACS=[NUM]
  @{b}Example:@{ub} PRIV.ACS=20

 This is the minimum security level a user needs to automatically
 gain priveliged access to noC-WHO.

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE opt4 "noC-WHO Configuration Option: DIVIDERS_DISABLED"

 @{b}Template: @{ub}DIVIDERS_DISABLED=[YES/NO]
  @{b}Example: @{ub}DIVIDERS_DISABLED=YES

 If you have more than 6 nodes or so, you may want to disable the dividing
 lines  between  node  texts to make the display more COMPACT.  If this is
 the case, set this parameter to YES.

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE opt5 "noC-WHO Configuration Option: CLEAR_SCREEN"

 @{b}Template: @{ub}CLEAR_SCREEN=[YES/NO]
  @{b}Example: @{ub}CLEAR_SCREEN=YES

 If  this  is  set  to  YES,  noC-WHO  will simply clear the screen before
 displaying the node information.

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE opt6 "noC-WHO Configuration Option: PUBLIC_UPDATE"

 @{b}Template: @{ub}PUBLIC_UPDATE=[YES/NO]
  @{b}Example: @{ub}PUBLIC_UPDATE=NO

 Some sysops may not want the general public to be able to use the 'WHO U'
 option  to  CONSTANTLY  MONITOR  other  nodes, and can use this option to
 DISABLE it.

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE opt7 "noC-WHO Configuration Option: COOLUSER.[NUM]"

 @{b}Template: @{ub}COOLUSER.[NUM]=[USERNAME]
  @{b}Example: @{ub}COOLUSER.1=nocTurne
           COOLUSER.2=OJ Simpson

 Trusted users should be listed with this parameter.  They will be able to
 see  EVERYTHING,  (quieted  nodes, etc...), and have privileged access to
 the  door.    As  each  user is added you must increment [NUM].  Up to 20
 users may be listed.

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE opt8 "noC-WHO Configuration Option: MISC.[NUM]"

 @{b}Template: @{ub}MISC.TITLE=[TEXT]
  @{b}Example: @{ub}MISC.TITLE=Phone #

 noC-WHO  features  a configurable column which you may use as you please. 
 (Ideas:  Phone  numbers,  modem  types,  node info, etc..) To define this
 field for your own purposes you must first create a title. 

 @{b}Template: @{ub}MISC.[NUM]=[TEXT]
  @{b}Example: @{ub}MISC.0=LOCAL
           MISC.1=555-1212
           MISC.2=555-1213

 You  may  define  what is printed in the configurable field of EACH NODE.
 If you want any of them to display the BAUD, set those to '@'.

 Please  note,  the  misc field is not used in some of the included design
 styles.

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE opt9 "noC-WHO Configuration Option: SYSOP_ACTION"

 @{b}Template: @{ub}SYSOP_ACTION=[TEXT]
  @{b}Example: @{ub}SYSOP_ACTION=Hanging in the John. 

 Some  sysops  like  to  have  themselves  listed  on  a local node.  This
 parameter  allows them to customize exactly what their ACTION field says.

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE opt10 "noC-WHO Configuration Option: YOURONE.TEXT"

 @{b}Template: @{ub}YOURNODE.TEXT=[TEXT]
  @{b}Example: @{ub}YOURNODE.TEXT=>> Your Node <<

 The  text  listed here will be used in place of the user's name, when HIS
 node is being shown to him.

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE opt11 "noC-WHO Configuration Option: SOMEONE.TEXT"

 @{b}Template: @{ub}SOMEONE.TEXT=[TEXT]
  @{b}Example: @{ub}SOMEONE.TEXT=New Caller

 This  text  will be used as the name of a person just logging in or who is
 registering with the system.

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE opt12 "noC-WHO Configuration Option: SOMEWHERE.TEXT"

 @{b}Template: @{ub}SOMEWHERE.TEXT=[TEXT]
  @{b}Example: @{ub}SOMEWHERE.TEXT=New Caller

 This  text  will be used as the location of a person just logging in or 
 who is registering with the system.

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE opt13 "noC-WHO Configuration Option: [DOOR].NAME"

 @{b}Template: @{ub}[DOOR].NAME=[TEXT]
  @{b}Example: @{ub}COUP.NAME=noC-Coupon V1.0
           N.NAME=NewFiles V2.0
          
 If  the  user  has  the  appropriate  access   level,   (defined   by the
 DOORNAMES.ACS  config  option),  he  will  see the raw names of any doors
 users  are  in.  (eg, those in a new-files door will have an action field
 of  'N').   However you may give more descriptive names to doors with the
 [DOOR].NAME feature.  The second example above shows how you can give the
 'N' door a more explicit label.

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE opt14 "noC-WHO Configuration Option: [CALL].STYLE"

 @{b}Template: @{ub}[CALL].STYLE=[NUM]
  @{b}Example: @{ub}FRONTEND.STYLE=10

 If  you  want  a  specific  style to be used when the door is called by a
 particular  name,  such as in the case of a login who display (FRONTEND),
 you may select the style to be used with this option.  In the example, if
 noC-WHO  is called  by the name FRONTEND, it will use the 10th design, (A
 compact  node  display).   If you want that users NOT have the ability to
 select  a  design,  you  can  SET  the style by using this command.  (ie,
 WHO.STYLE=1)

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE opt15 "noC-WHO Configuration Option: TITLE.[NUM]"

 @{b}Template: @{ub}TITLE.[NUM]=[TEXT]
  @{b}Example: @{ub}TITLE.1=Simple Design

 Use  this  string to designate a NAME for a particular who design.  [NUM]
 may  be  a  number from 1 to 9, as you may have up to 9 screen styles for
 the  user  to  select  from.    The  title  will  be   shown   during the
 CONFIGURATION  session  and  should  convey  the 'mood' of its respective
 design, while remaining under 18 characters.

@ENDNODE 
*******************************************************************************
@NODE opt16 "noC-WHO Configuration Option: STYLE_FILE.[NUM]"

 @{b}Template: @{ub}STYLE_FILE.[NUM]=[PATH+FILENAME]
  @{b}Example: @{ub}STYLE_FILE.1=doors:noC-WHO/styles/simple.style

 This  parameter should be set to the filename of a style-file you wish to
 use for slot [NUM] and should be used in conjunction with the TITLE.[NUM]
 parameter.    There  are  up  to 9 slots available for the user to choose
 from;  those  after 9 are only accessible with the [DOOR].STYLE option.  
 You  must increment [NUM] each time you add a style-file entry, and there
 must  be  no skips.  (ie, if you intend to have 4 who styles for users to
 be  able  to choose from, you must use slots 1,2,3 and 4; not 1,5,8 and 9
 or such.)

 Please reference the included config file for more information.

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE opt17 "noC-WHO Configuration Option: @BEGIN_ACTIONS"

 If  you  want  to  customize  the  action  texts  you  can edit the texts
 proceeding  the  "@BEGIN_ACTIONS"  string  in  the  included config file.
 These  are  the default texts for every action, (Awaiting call, Beginning
 UL,  etc...).   When you EDIT these texts, be sure to keep them around 16
 characters  long  and  KEEP  THEM IN THE ORDER THEY CAME.  Any additional
 carriage returns or alterations to the arrangement of the file may result
 in incorrect action displays.

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE opt18 "noC-WHO Configuration Option: @END_ACTIONS"

 This string TERMINATES the action text list.

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE optA "noC-WHO Configuration Option: SHOW_QUIET"

 @{b}Template: @{ub}SHOW_QUIET=[YES/NO]
  @{b}Example: @{ub}SHOW_QUIET=YES

 If you wish underpriveliged users to be able to see when a node is
 hidden,  (no name  or  location  is  shown, only the action in the
 @{"QUIETACTION.TEXT" link optB} config parameter), set this option.

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE optB "noC-WHO Configuration Option: QUIETACTION.TEXT"

 @{b}Template: @{ub}QUIETACTION.TEXT=[TEXT]
  @{b}Example: @{ub}QUIETACTION.TEXT=Awaiting Call

 This text will be the action text of nodes that are quietened (to
 those that dont have priveliged access to the door). You may wish
 to use the example above,  if you want quiet nodes to simply look
 like available nodes.

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE optC "noC-WHO Configuration Option: SHOW_EMPTY_NODES"

 @{b}Template: @{ub}SHOW_EMPTY_NODES=[YES/NO]
  @{b}Example: @{ub}SHOW_EMPTY_NODES=YES

 If this is set to NO, those nodes that are unoccupied will not
 be shown.

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE STYCONT "noC-WHO Style-File Contents..."

 Each  style-file  is  required to have the following four sections, which
 will  be  used  to draw the who-display.  Each section has its own set of
 '@'  commands  available.   For more information regarding that, read the
 @{"Command Usage" link CMDUSE} section.
 
 -------------------------------------------------------------------------
 @{"       HEAD       " link HEAD}@{"       BODY       " link BODY}@{"      DIVIDER      " link DIVIDER}@{"      TAIL      " link TAIL}
 -------------------------------------------------------------------------

@ENDNODE 
****************************************************************************** 
@NODE HEAD "noC-WHO Style-File Section: HEAD"

 @{b}Template: @{ub}HEAD=[TEXT]%
  @{b}Example: 
 @{b}-----------------------8< Cut Here --------------------------------------@{ub}
 HEAD=
 =========================================================
 NODE      NAME                      @MISC..........  CONF
 =========================================================
 %
 @{b}-----------------------8< Cut Here --------------------------------------@{ub}

 This  defines  the  HEADER for a style-file.  This text will comprise the
 top  part  of the who screen and may be used to title the fields you will
 define  in the "BODY" section, or it may simply be an interesting looking
 design/logo  to preceed the node texts.  ANSI codes may be used anywhere.
 Look  at  one  of the examples included for more examples.  Note that the
 text  may  have  as  many  carriage  returns  as  you  like,  but must be
 TERMINATED by a '%' sign.

 See @{"Header Commands" link HEADCMDS} for the '@' commands available.
@ENDNODE 
*******************************************************************************
@NODE BODY "noC-WHO Style-File Section: BODY"

 @{b}Template: @{ub}BODY=[TEXT]%
  @{b}Example: 
 @{b}-----------------------8< Cut Here --------------------------------------@{ub}
 BODY=
  @#       @NAME.................... @MISC..........   @$
 %
 @{b}-----------------------8< Cut Here --------------------------------------@{ub}
 
 The  text  written  after  this argument will compose the BODY of the who
 display.    This  text  will  repeatedly  be  displayed for each node and
 information  will  be  filled  in  accordingly.    As with all style-file
 sections,  the body-text must be terminated by a '%', and may be composed
 of any number of ANSI codes.

 See @{"Body Commands" link HEADCMDS} for the '@' commands available.

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE DIVIDER "noC-WHO Style-File Section: DIVIDER.[NUM]"

 @{b}Template: @{ub}DIVIDER=[TEXT]%
  @{b}Example: 
 @{b}-----------------------8< Cut Here --------------------------------------@{ub}
 DIVIDER=
 (======================================================)
 %
 @{b}-----------------------8< Cut Here --------------------------------------@{ub}

 If  you  have  node dividers enabled, the text written here will separate
 each  of  the  body(node)  texts.    You  may use as many rows of text to
 seperate  the  node  informations; however, one row is suggested.  Again,
 the text must end with a '%' and may be composed of ANSI sequences.

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE TAIL "noC-WHO Style-File Section: TAIL"

 @{b}Template: @{ub}TAIL=[TEXT]%
  @{b}Example: 
 @{b}-----------------------8< Cut Here --------------------------------------@{ub}
 TAIL.1=
 (======================================================)
  @VERSIONTEXT - (C)1994 nocTurne deSign - Time: @TIME
 %
 @{b}-----------------------8< Cut Here --------------------------------------@{ub}

 This  text  will  be the last string emitted.  You may use it to show the
 time, the number of calls for the day, or to give yourself credit for the
 design  you created.  Any number of lines may be used, ANSI sequences are
 allowed, and the text must be terminated with a '%'.

 See @{"Tail Commands" link HEADCMDS} for the '@' commands available.

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE USAGE "noC-WHO Usage..."

 Information  on program usage may be obtained with "WHO ?".  To configure
 noC-WHO to one of the nine (or less) styles available, the user may enter
 "WHO E".  This will engage the preferences mode, where the person may use
 his  cursor  or  numeric   keys  to   select  a  style  of  his   choice.  
 Additionally,  in the preferences, if a user has privileged access to the
 door,  he  may  edit  his name, location, or action fields to whatever he
 likes.   Setting these fields to nothing will make their node fields show
 to  others normally.  A data file with all the users' preferences will be
 kept named "DOORS:noC-WHO/data/who-users.dat".

 If  the UPDATE option has been made available to all users, or the user is
 privileged,  he may use this door to actively monitor the actions of those
 online.    "WHO  U"  will cause noC-WHO to update its display every second
 until  a  key  is pressed.  If the "PUBLIC_UPDATE" config option is set to
 "NO",  only  privileged users will be able to have display updates.  Those
 with  privileged  access  may  also  set their ACTION text.  Setting it to
 NOTHING will cause all actions to be displayed normally.

 Nodes   that   are   QUIETED   are   EXEMPT   from   being   displayed to
 underpriviledged  users.  To priviliged  users, quiet  nodes have actions
 enclosed in parentheses.  Nodes  that are disabled or unavailable will be
 shown  as  such.   At midnight, a task spawned by the door upon its first
 run will AUTOMATICALLY RESET the call counter to 0.

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE STYLE "Creating Custom noC-WHO Screens..."

  The  following  entries  describe the construct of  noC-WHO style-files. 
  These  files let  you  design  a  who look of your own with a minimum of
  effort.  Reference the included .style-files for more information.
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  @{" Style-File Contents  " link STYCONT }.....................Divisions of the style-files
  @{" Command Set Usage    " link CMDUSE  }......................How to use the '@' commands
  @{" Header/Tail Commands " link HEADCMDS}........Descriptions of header/tail text commands
  @{" Body Commands        " link BODYCMDS}...............Descriptions of body text commands
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------
  
@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE CMDUSE "noC-WHO Command Set Utilization..."

 noC-WHO  features  an  '@' command set, (each command begins with the '@'
 character),  which  may  be  used  to  call up particular strings in your
 header, body, and tail texts.  This is best explained with examples:

 Take  the "@TIME" command.  When noC-WHO finds this text in the header or
 tail texts, it will replace it with the current system time.

 If your tail text in the style-file reads:
 @{b}---------------------------------------------------8< Cut Here ----------@{ub}
 ===============================================================
  @VERSIONTEXT - This WHO-Door Smells - The Time: @TIME
 @{b}---------------------------------------------------8< Cut Here ----------@{ub}

 When displayed it would read:
 @{b}---------------------------------------------------8< Cut Here ----------@{ub}
 ===============================================================
  noC-WHO V1.6 - This WHO-Door Smells - The Time: 01:20
 @{b}---------------------------------------------------8< Cut Here ----------@{ub}

 You  may use  periods  to extend the length of the text.  For example, to
 display the system time WITH seconds:

 "The Time: @TIME..."-----=would yield=----->"The Time: 01:20:12"

 An example from the body-text command set; if this were your body string:

 @{b}----------------------------------------------8< Cut Here ---------------@{ub}
  @# | @NAME............... | @LOC................... | @ACTION.......
 @{b}----------------------------------------------8< Cut Here ---------------@{ub}

 The output text (body only, for three nodes) would then be:

 @{b}----------------------------------------------8< Cut Here ---------------@{ub}
  01 | nocTurne             | ·-÷« ORIGIN »÷-·        | NUN'A'YOR'BIZ!
  02 |                      |                         | Awaiting Call 
  03 | Jake Steinfeld       | From body-building heav | Viewing Dirs  
 @{b}----------------------------------------------8< Cut Here ---------------@{ub}

 As  you  can see from node 3, where there are no periods the text will be
 cut  off.    If  you only used "@NAME", without any of the periods, names
 would extend no longer than 5 characters.

 This concludes your lesson of the noC-WHO command-set.

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE HEADCMDS "noC-WHO Header/Tail Commands..."

                     4 Header/Tail Commands Available:
                     ---------------------------------
                          @{"         @CL          " link cmd1}
                          @{"        @MISC         " link cmd2}
                          @{"        @TIME         " link cmd3}
                          @{"     @VERSIONTEXT     " link cmd4}
                     ---------------------------------
@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE BODYCMDS "noC-WHO Body Commands..."

                       8 Body Commands Available:
                       --------------------------
                               @{" @#      " link cmd5}
                               @{" @$      " link cmd6}
                               @{" @ACTION " link cmd7}
                               @{" @BAUD   " link cmd8}
                               @{" @LOC    " link cmd9}
                               @{" @MISC   " link cmd10}
                               @{" @NAME   " link cmd11}
                               @{" @WHEN   " link cmd12}
                       --------------------------
@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE cmd1 "noC-WHO Header/Tail Command: @CL"

  @{b}Command: @{ub}@CL
   @{b}Result: @{ub}Number of calls today

 If  you expect more than a thousand calls in one day (!), you may want to
 extend the field to 4 characters, ("@CL.").

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE cmd2 "noC-WHO Header/Tail Command: @MISC"

  @{b}Command: @{ub}@MISC
   @{b}Result: @{ub}Text defined in MISC.TITLE config parameter

 This  string  will be  replaced with the title of the miscellaneous field
 you've defined.  ie, whatever has been defined in the @{" MISC.TITLE " link opt8} config
 argument will be displayed here.

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE cmd3 "noC-WHO Header/Tail Command: @TIME"

  @{b}Command: @{ub}@TIME
   @{b}Result: @{ub}System time

 By  default, a five character time display will replace the command text,
 ("@TIME"  is five characters long).  Thus only the hours and seconds will
 be shown.  Extending the field to eight characters will show the complete
 system time, (ie "@TIME..."--->"01:12:20").

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE cmd4 "noC-WHO Header/Tail Command: @VERSIONTEXT"

  @{b}Command: @{ub}@VERSIONTEXT
   @{b}Result: @{ub}"noC-WHO V1.6" string

 This command  text  will  be replaced with the version string of noC-WHO.
 THIS  COMMAND  MUST  BE SOMEWHERE  IN  YOUR  HEADER/TAIL  TEXT or else an
 annoying  version string will be appended to the who display.  Be warned. 

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE cmd5 "noC-WHO Body Command: @#"

  @{b}Command: @{ub}@#
   @{b}Result: @{ub}Current node number being showed

 This is simply a two character field that will be replaced with the number
 of  the  NODE  that  is currently being displayed.  As each node is shown,
 this number will be incremented respectively.

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE cmd6 "noC-WHO Body Command: @$"

  @{b}Command: @{ub}@$
   @{b}Result: @{ub}User's current conference

 This command will show the current node's user CONFERENCE NUMBER.  Please
 note,  in  order for this to function correctly you must have the CALLERS
 LOG  for each node turned on for this to function.  noC-WHO will use data
 buffering  methods  to  use  as  little  disk I/O as possible for maximum
 speed.

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE cmd7 "noC-WHO Body Command: @ACTION"

  @{b}Command: @{ub}@ACTION
   @{b}Result: @{ub}What the user is doing

 This string  will reflect what ACTION the user of the node being shown is
 taking.    It is  suggested  you extend the field to around 18 characters
 length, (ie "@ACTION...........").

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE cmd8 "noC-WHO Body Command: @BAUD"

  @{b}Command: @{ub}@BAUD
   @{b}Result: @{ub}Users connect rate

 This string  will  be  replaced with the BAUD of the user currently being
 shown.

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE cmd9 "noC-WHO Body Command: @LOC"

  @{b}Command: @{ub}@LOC
   @{b}Result: @{ub}User's Location/Group

 This field  should be extended to around 16 characters to fit the average
 user's location line, (ie, "@LOC............")

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE cmd10 "noC-WHO Body Command: @MISC"

  @{b}Command: @{ub}@MISC
   @{b}Result: @{ub}Misc field definition for current node

 This command will show what is defined in the @{"MISC" link opt8} field for each node.

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE cmd11 "noC-WHO Body Command: @NAME"

  @{b}Command: @{ub}@NAME
   @{b}Result: @{ub}The user's name/handle

 To show  most user's full names/handles, this field should be extended to
 around 14 characters, (ie, "@NAME.........").

@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
@NODE cmd12 "noC-WHO Body Command: @WHEN"

  @{b}Command: @{ub}@WHEN
   @{b}Result: @{ub}User's time of login

 This  will  show  the time,  (hours  and minutes), of the currently shown
 user's login.  If you wish seconds to be shown as well, this field should
 be extended to 8 characters, (ie, "@WHEN...").  You must have the callers
 log turned on for this to function.
  
@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************

@NODE CHANGES "noC-WHO Changes..."

 @{b}Changes from Version 1.5 to 1.6:@{ub}
 
 · Added buffering routines so that the callerslog would be accessed
   as little as possible.  
 · Other routine optimizations/speed increases.
 · Styles are now external files, and are no longer within the config file.
 · No longer uses the doorlog to obtain doornames
 · Added three access level based configuration settings and four
   other options.
 · Privileged users can now see the actions of those on quiet nodes
   and edit their own name and location field.
 · Other minor structural changes.
 
@ENDNODE
*******************************************************************************
